# Caddy with the Vultr DNS provider compiled in.
#
# Caddy's DNS providers are compile-time modules and the stock image ships none,
# so DNS-01 is impossible with `caddy:2-alpine` however the Caddyfile is written.
# That is the only reason this file exists.
#
# DNS-01 is what a wildcard needs. HTTP-01 proves control of one hostname by
# serving a file at it, which cannot prove control of names that do not exist
# yet, and a wildcard authorises exactly those. Proving control of the zone means
# writing a TXT record, which means an API token, which means this binary.
#
# Building this on the game host was the alternative, and it is the arrangement
# we just finished deleting: a compiler on a public-facing box, ten minutes of
# first boot, and a class of failure nobody can debug without a shell.
FROM caddy:2-builder AS build
RUN xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/vultr

FROM caddy:2-alpine
COPY --from=build /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy

# Fail the build rather than ship a Caddy that silently cannot do DNS-01. An
# xcaddy step that half-worked would produce a binary that parses `dns vultr` as
# an unknown directive and falls back to HTTP-01, and the way we would find out
# is a wildcard issuance failing against the rate limit -- which is the one
# outcome this whole exercise exists to avoid.
RUN caddy list-modules | grep -qx 'dns.providers.vultr' \
 && echo 'dns.providers.vultr present'
